<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/the-novel-a-short-history">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The novel is <strong>400 years old and now the world's dominant literary form</strong> — born
    from parody, raised by print and the middle class, matured into the great psychological and
    social instrument of modern literature. Its history is a series of technical inventions
    (free indirect style, stream of consciousness, unreliable narrators) that expanded what prose
    could hold.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four centuries of invention, from Quixote to magical realism" widget="equationStep" config={novelCfg} />

  <h2>The technical inventions that made the form</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Free indirect style (Austen's invention, arguably):</strong> third-person narration sliding into a character's thoughts without quotation — "She felt sure that he was lying" carries her judgement inside the narrator's voice. The 19th century's quiet revolution.</li>
    <li><strong>Unreliable narration:</strong> narrators who lie or mislead (Nabokov's Humbert, Kazuo Ishiguro's butler) — the reader becomes a diagnostician, reading against the text.</li>
    <li><strong>Stream of consciousness:</strong> Woolf, Joyce, Faulkner rendering thought as it actually flows — associative, unpunctuated, pre-verbal. Ulysses (1922) remade the novel's contract with the mind.</li>
    <li><strong>The serial and the market:</strong> Dickens published in monthly parts — cliffhangers before television. The novel's forms were always shaped by its publishing economics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Watt, The Rise of the Novel (1957); Forster, Aspects of the Novel (1927); the primary novels themselves.</p>
</TopicPage>
